    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Radiomics aims to radically improve cancer detection through automating the evaluation of potentially cancerous masses in medical images. Early evaluation of cancer is crucial to treatment decisions and to eventual survival outcomes. Radiomics approaches compute quantitative descriptors (shape, size, intensity, texture, etc.) of regions of interest in images and then build predictive statistical models from these numerical data. Computational techniques require radiologists to define regions of interest (ROI) in a process called segmentation. Radiologists often disagree on the exact outline of ROIs, which leads to unreliable data and poor models. We propose a new paradigm called “radiomic biopsies” that focuses on a spherical sample of the ROI. For intensity and texture features robust to variation in segmentation, radiomic biopsies produce samples of these features without the laborious segmentation process. Radiomic biopsies have proven to produce reliable features, but their application to predictive modeling has never been tested. To explore the application of radiomic biopsies to different disease types, we conducted a simulated case study in predicting non-small-cell lung carcinoma recurrence from chest CT scans. We then designed and implemented a novel 3D Radiomic Biopsy Tool for differentiating oncocytoma and chromophobe renal cell carcinoma. Across both studies, we showed that radiomic biopsies perform similarly to full segmentations on proven predictive tasks and can successfully differentiate subtypes in novel tasks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
